    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Did these attention and restlessness problems start before age 12?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Yeah, definitely way before I was 12. Even back in elementary school, my teachers were always writing comments on my report cards about me rushing through my worksheets and completely losing track of directions. Which, speaking of worksheets, do you remember those timed multiplication things? The \"Mad Minute\" ones? I hated those so much. I always wanted to use my sparkly gel pens for them, but my third-grade teacher, Mrs. Gable, made us use regular number two pencils. She was so obsessed with Ticonderoga pencils. Like, she literally had a giant cardboard cutout of a yellow pencil on her classroom door. I wonder if they still make those gel pens, actually... the ones that smelled like fruit? I had a strawberry one that I kept in this little fuzzy pencil case, and I took it everywhere until it leaked all over my backpack. \n\nWait, sorry, what was I talking about? Oh, right, the teacher comments! Yeah, they also complained that I was always talking over my classmates during group work. And at home, it was exactly the same. My parents used to have to sit right beside me at the kitchen table just so I'd do my homework, because otherwise I would completely wander off or start something else before finishing. Like, I'd go to the kitchen for a snack and end up trying to build a fort out of the living room sofa cushions. So, yeah, it's pretty much always been like this.",
